[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m user
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m You have seven bags of gold coins. Each bag has the same number of gold coins. One day, you find a bag of 53 coins. You decide to redistribute the number of coins you have so that all eight bags you hold have the same number of coins. You successfully manage to redistribute all the coins, and you also note that you have more than 200 coins. What is the smallest number of coins you could have had before finding the bag of 53 coins?
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{}.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m assistant
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m output:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m Let's denote the number of coins in each of the original six bags by \( x \). Thus, initially, the total number of coins is \( 7x \). After finding the bag of 53 coins, the total number of coins becomes \( 7x + 53 \). We want to redistribute these coins so that all eight bags have the same number of coins, which we will call \( y \). Therefore, the total number of coins must be divisible by 8, and we can write:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m \[ 7x + 53 = 8y \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m This means that \( 7x + 53 \) is a multiple of 8. We can rearrange this equation to solve for \( x \):
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m \[ 7x + 53 \equiv 0 \pmod{8} \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m \[ 7x \equiv -53 \pmod{8} \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m \[ 7x \equiv -53 + 64 \pmod{8} \text{ (since } -53 \div 8 \text{ has a remainder of } 3) \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m \[ 7x \equiv 11 \pmod{8} \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m \[ 7x \equiv 3 \pmod{8} \text{ (since } 11 \div 8 \text{ has a remainder of } 3) \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m Now, we need to find the multiplicative inverse of 7 modulo 8. Since \( 7 \times 7 = 49 \equiv 1 \pmod{8} \), the inverse of 7 modulo 8 is 7. Multiplying both sides of the congruence by 7 gives:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m \[ x \equiv 3 \times 7 \pmod{8} \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m \[ x \equiv 21 \pmod{8} \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m \[ x \equiv 5 \pmod{8} \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m Therefore, the smallest positive integer value for \( x \) that satisfies this congruence is \( x = 5 \). Substituting \( x = 5 \) back into the expression for the total number of coins, we get:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m \[ 7x + 53 = 7(5) + 53 = 35 + 53 = 88 \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m To check if we have more than 200 coins, let's see if there's a smaller solution for \( x \) that meets our condition. We can systematically check the values of \( x \) in the form \( x = 5 + 8k \) for non-negative integers \( k \):
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m For \( k = 0 \):
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m \[ x = 5 \implies 7(5) + 53 = 88 \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m For \( k = 1 \):
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m \[ x = 13 \implies 7(13) + 53 = 94 \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m For \( k = 2 \):
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m \[ x = 21 \implies 7(21) + 53 = 166 \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m For \( k = 3 \):
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m \[ x = 29 \implies 7(29) + 53 = 234 \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m And so on...
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m 
