[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m To determine the smallest number of coins you could have had before finding the bag of 53 coins, we start by defining the initial number of coins in each of the seven bags as \( n \). Thus, the total number of coins initially is \( 7n \).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m After finding the bag of 53 coins, you have a total of \( 7n + 53 \) coins. You then redistribute these coins into eight bags so that all bags have the same number of coins. Let \( k \) be the number of coins in each bag after redistribution. Therefore, the equation for the total number of coins after redistribution is:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m \[ 8k = 7n + 53. \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m This implies that \( 7n + 53 \) must be divisible by 8. Hence, we can write:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m \[ 7n + 53 \equiv 0 \pmod{8}, \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m which simplifies to:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m \[ 7n + 53 \equiv 7n + 53 - 64 \equiv 7n - 1 \equiv 0 \pmod{8}, \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m or
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m \[ 7n \equiv 1 \pmod{8}. \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m To solve for \( n \), we need the multiplicative inverse of 7 modulo 8. Since \( 7 \equiv -1 \pmod{8} \), the inverse of 7 modulo 8 is the number \( x \) such that:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m \[ -x \equiv 1 \pmod{8}, \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m which means:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m \[ x \equiv -1 \pmod{8} \implies x \equiv 7 \pmod{8}. \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m Thus, the inverse of 7 modulo 8 is 7, and we can rewrite the congruence \( 7n \equiv 1 \pmod{8} \) as:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m \[ n \equiv 7 \pmod{8}. \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m Therefore, \( n \) can be written as:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m \[ n = 8m + 7 \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m for some integer \( m \). The total number of coins before finding the bag of 53 coins is:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m \[ 7n = 7(8m + 7) = 56m + 49. \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m We also know that after redistributing, you have more than 200 coins, i.e.,
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m \[ 8k = 7n + 53 > 200. \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m Substituting \( n = 8m + 7 \) into the inequality gives:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m \[ 8k = 7(8m + 7) + 53 = 56m + 49 + 53 = 56m + 102. \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m So,
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m \[ 8k > 200 \implies k > 25. \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m The smallest value of \( k \) greater than 25 is 26. Substituting \( k = 26 \) into the equation \( 8k = 56m + 102 \) gives:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m \[ 8 \times 26 = 56m + 102 \implies 208 = 56m + 102 \implies 56m = 106 \implies m = \frac{106}{56} = \frac{53}{28}, \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m which is an integer when rounded, or directly,
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m \[ 208 = 56m + 102 \implies 56m = 106 \implies m = \frac{106}{56} = \frac{53}{28} = \frac{53}{7}. \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m Hence,
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m \[ m = 1 \implies n = 8(1) + 7 = 15, \text{and } 7n = 7(15) = 105 \text{ gives } 8k = 105 + 53 = 158 \text{and the next smallest value is when } m = 2, \text{but gives 7n = } 7(2) + 7 = 21 \text{so } n = 28, \text{and it's smallest }. \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m To summarize we choose:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=3356756)[0m \[ m=1 \implies n = 15, \text{and } 7n = 105 > 200 \text{not meeting criteria thus smallest ] while,
